{
  "rationale": "This is iteration 0, so there is no race telemetry and no Integrator feedback yet. The design therefore rests on the contract alone. Five sensors spaced 10 mm apart across x (-0.02 to +0.02 m) cover the 25 mm tape with overlapping 12 mm footprints. Each sensor always sees some tape within a 25 mm line, so the reading changes smoothly as the line moves sideways, and the Integrator gets a fine-grained, symmetric error signal. The sensors sit 0.05 m ahead of the axle so the robot sees the line before the wheels reach it, which gives time to correct at speed. Wheel track of 0.12 m and radius of 0.03 m give a moderate turn authority (turn rate ≈ 2·v/track in the differential case), and a 0.8 kg mass with a 0.16 m body keeps yaw inertia modest for quick corrections. Body width does not change the off-track limit at these sizes, because tape_half_width + bw/4 stays below the 0.06 m floor until bw is about 0.19 m, so 0.12 m is simply a neutral choice. I expect a first race to complete the oval and s-bend if the Integrator's controller holds the line; the main unknowns are speed-induced line loss on the slalom and whether 5 sensors at this spacing are enough for the sharpest bends.",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": -0.02, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.01, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.01, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.02, "y": 0.05}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.012,
    "wheel_track": 0.12,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "body_width": 0.12,
    "body_length": 0.16,
    "mass": 0.8
  },
  "intent": "Establish a symmetric, well-resolved five-sensor baseline with a forward preview row. Sensor order runs from right (negative x) to left (positive x), so the Integrator can assign antisymmetric weights, for example [-2,-1,0,1,2], with positive signal steering left. The spacing and footprint overlap keep the signal continuous as the line crosses the array. The moderate track and mass should allow speed without excessive yaw inertia. Next iteration, I will tune spacing, the forward row distance and the wheel track using the Integrator's feedback and the race telemetry.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "questions": [
      "Does a 12 mm footprint with 10 mm spacing give a smooth, non-saturating signal across the 25 mm tape, or is the array too coarse or too wide at the centre?",
      "Is the 0.05 m forward row far enough ahead to correct before the axle reaches a bend, or should sensors move forward or split into two rows?",
      "Does the 0.12 m wheel track give enough turn authority for the slalom without causing oscillation?",
      "Would a different sensor count (3 or 7) or spacing make the controller simpler or more robust?"
    ]
  },
  "learning": "No race data yet. Baseline hypothesis: a symmetric five-sensor array with 12 mm footprints and a forward preview row is adequate for the oval and s-bend, and the slalom will be the first failure point. Revisit once telemetry is available."
}
